fe.add_exercise(S, 'swap the storage behind the same interface',
"""`createRepository()` returns an object with `query`, `find`, `create`, `update`, `setStatus`, `remove`, `listActivity`, `addActivity`. Re-implement that object over `node:sqlite` (or a JSON file with atomic rename) without changing the routes.

1. Write the schema (`items`, `activity`) and a `createSqliteRepository(path)` with the same eight methods.
2. `setStatus` must keep its two guarantees: the equality guard (no write on a repeat) and the paired record + history write, now inside a `BEGIN`/`COMMIT` (or a single atomic file replace).
3. Switch the app to the new repository behind an environment variable and run the existing `test/workflow.test.js` unchanged.

Acceptance: every existing assertion passes against the new storage; a restart keeps the data (add a test that reopens the store and finds the record); and the third test's "one status entry after two requests" still holds.""",
"""Reference shape for `setStatus` over `node:sqlite`:

```js
setStatus(id, status) {
  return tx(() => {
    const existing = db.prepare("SELECT * FROM items WHERE id = ?").get(id);
    if (!existing) return null;
    if (existing.status === status) return existing;
    db.prepare("UPDATE items SET status = ?, updatedAt = ? WHERE id = ?").run(status, now(), id);
    db.prepare("INSERT INTO activity (id, itemId, kind, body, createdAt) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)").run(crypto.randomUUID(), id, "status", `Changed status from ${existing.status} to ${status}`, now());
    return db.prepare("SELECT * FROM items WHERE id = ?").get(id);
  });
}
```

Review: the interface is the contract; the tests are the proof that the contract held. Explain which guarantee you had for free with the array (no interleaving) and which mechanism replaced it (the transaction)."""
, number=7); done.append('review#6 exercise 7: swap the in-memory repository for SQLite behind the same interface (05, 06)')
